From: HarmonyOS PC adaptation <adapt@example.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] uucpsend.c: fix free_sites() parameter type and lvalue cast

`free_sites()` is written as

    void
    free_sites(root)
    struct s_sites *root;      /* <-- struct s_sites is never declared */
    {
      struct s_site *act;
      (struct s_sites *)act = (struct s_sites *)root;

`struct s_sites` does not exist anywhere in the program -- only
`struct s_site` does.  Because the parameter uses an undeclared struct tag,
C gives it a type that is incomplete and local to this function, so:

  * `(struct s_sites *)act = ...` is an assignment to a cast, which is not a
    modifiable lvalue.  GCC historically accepted it with a warning, but
    clang reports a hard error:

        uucpsend.c:288:3: error: assignment to cast is illegal,
                          lvalue casts are not supported

  * every call site passes a `struct s_site *`, which does not match the
    declared parameter type, producing -Wincompatible-pointer-types.

Both are the same root cause.  Declaring the parameter as `struct s_site *`
and assigning directly fixes the compile error and removes the three
incompatible-pointer-types diagnostics at the call sites, without changing
behaviour: the function only walks the list through `->next` and frees the
`name`/`header`/`compressor`/`uuxargs` members, all of which are declared on
`struct s_site`.

This is the same correction Debian carries as
0002-uucpsend.c-Don-t-cast-lvalue-in-assignment.patch (Andreas Jochens,
Debian bug #297738), arrived at independently from the compiler diagnostic.

Upstream-Status: Submitted [portability]
Signed-off-by: HarmonyOS PC adaptation <adapt@example.invalid>
---
--- a/uucpsend.c
+++ b/uucpsend.c
@@ -280,12 +280,12 @@
 
 void
 free_sites(root)
-struct s_sites *root;
+struct s_site *root;
 {
   struct s_site *act;
   struct s_site *tmp = NULL;
 
-  (struct s_sites *)act = (struct s_sites *)root;
+  act = root;
 
   while (act) {
     tmp = act;
